"A system landscape locates software systems and their relationships across a bounded organizational scope.\n\nUse when: Survey applications across a department or product portfolio. Find integration ownership before choosing a system to inspect.\n\nAvoid when: A single request or internal implementation needs explanation.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes denote systems and user roles, with ownership stated in subtitles. Directed edges carry short interaction verbs; boundaries denote the declared organizational scope. A legend separates observed integrations from proposals and unknown connections.\n\nBuild: Choose an explicit organizational scope and observation date. List systems from an inventory, resolving aliases before drawing. Place heavily connected systems centrally and cluster by ownership. Route integrations in separate corridors; pad group boundaries and reserve label space.\n\nCheck: Every edge identifies its source, target, and purpose. Systems stay at one abstraction level without databases mixed in as applications. Proposed links are visibly distinguished and evidence is recorded. No connector intersects a system name or boundary title.\n\nPitfalls: Treating the landscape as an organizational hierarchy. Including every interface until the map becomes unreadable.\n\nExample: A retailer maps Storefront, Inventory, Billing, and Support, showing who owns each integration.\n\nAlternatives: System context diagram, Service dependency map."
